1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:05,200
Your app might run on the iPhone Duo, but that 
doesn't mean it's ready. There's a huge difference  

2
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:10,240
between an app that works on the iPhone Duo and 
one that feels specifically designed for it. So,  

3
00:00:10,240 --> 00:00:14,480
what do you need to do to get your app ready? 
That's what I'm breaking down in this video,  

4
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:18,480
including something cool about the hinge 
that opens up opportunities for brand new  

5
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:22,480
app ideas. All right, let's start with the big 
one. Your app is going to have six brand new  

6
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:26,560
layout configurations to support with the 
iPhone Duo, but you don't build six different  

7
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:30,880
UIs for your app. You're going to support the 
same size classes you may already be familiar  

8
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:35,440
with with compact and regular. And the iPhone 
Duo has various combinations of these. There's  

9
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:40,240
the compact width, regular height, which is the 
typical iPhone combination. But there's a new one,  

10
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:44,240
compact compact. When the iPhone Duo is closed 
and in landscape, that's going to be a new one  

11
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:49,360
we have to support. And when the Duo is unfolded, 
we get that regular regular size class combination  

12
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:53,520
that we're already used to with the iPad. 
So, if your app already fully supports iPad,  

13
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:58,640
you're going to be most of the way there. Now, 
I want to be clear. I said fully supports iPad.  

14
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:05,040
That means multitasking, resizability, multiple 
instances of your app, landscape, split view,  

15
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:10,960
and I'd argue that even though most apps work on 
the iPad, fully supporting the iPad is a different  

16
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:15,120
story. So, you're probably going to have some work 
to do here because the last thing you want is just  

17
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:19,920
a stretched out version of your iPhone app when 
the user unfolds the Duo. So to implement these  

18
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:23,600
changes, you're going to want to check the 
environment values for horizontal size class  

19
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:28,640
and vertical size class and then adapt your UI 
accordingly. For example, if on a typical iPhone  

20
00:01:28,640 --> 00:01:31,760
layout, you have views that are vertically 
stacked on top of each other, maybe you flip  

21
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:36,320
that to horizontally stacked when you're in the 
regular regular size class configuration. Or you  

22
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:40,480
can use split view to flatten out your navigation 
hierarchy if that makes sense for your app or use  

23
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:44,720
the sidebar configuration for your tab view if 
it's information dense. So, if you've been hard-  

24
00:01:44,720 --> 00:01:48,800
coding view widths in your app, or maybe you 
disabled landscape mode because you just didn't  

25
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:53,840
want to deal with that, not anymore. It's time 
to fully support that. But on the bright side,  

26
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:58,160
in fully supporting all this, the multitasking, 
the split views, the multiple instances of your  

27
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:02,400
app, landscape mode, you're going to end up with 
a really good, fully supported iPad app. So,  

28
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:07,520
it's kind of like a two for one deal. The iPhone 
Duo Simulator is shipping with Xcode 27.1,  

29
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:11,520
which Apple says will ship in late September 
of 2026. So, if you're watching this before,  

30
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:15,120
then you're going to have to wait a little bit 
before you can fully test out all these APIs and  

31
00:02:15,120 --> 00:02:20,720
see what your app really looks like on the Duo 
simulator. Xcode 27.1 also introduces a new AI  

32
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:25,040
agent skill for resizability. So, that should 
help adapt your app for the iPhone Duo. And  

33
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:30,800
this is what apps are going to look like that 
are not built with iOS 27 SDK or above. Not a  

34
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:35,200
great look. So, older apps that are no longer 
updated are really going to stand out on this  

35
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:38,960
device. So hopefully some of your competitors 
are doing that, but you you need to build on  

36
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:43,440
the iOS 27 SDK and above and your app will start 
looking like this. Start looking like it actually  

37
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:46,880
supports the iPhone Duo. Of course, you may have 
to do a little more work to make it look great,  

38
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:51,760
but building for the iOS 27 SDK is going to be a 
great start. Now, let's talk about your toolbars  

39
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:56,160
and tab views because they're going to have to 
adjust from the horizontal layout to the vertical  

40
00:02:56,160 --> 00:03:01,120
layout and back on the fly depending on what 
configuration your user has their iPhone Duo in.  

41
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:05,840
And the biggest tip here, which is what I've been 
saying for years, for situations just like this,  

42
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:10,400
use the native components, use built-in 
navigation stack, use the default tab view,  

43
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:14,480
use the built-in toolbar API. When you use 
these, most of the work to support the iPhone  

44
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:20,000
Duo is already done for you. But if you built your 
own custom toolbar or your own custom tab view,  

45
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,840
you're you're going to have a lot of work ahead 
of you. And I just recommend during that work,  

46
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:28,160
just switch to the native components. You're going 
to save yourself so much time and headache. And  

47
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:32,320
honestly, with liquid glass, they look great. 
But of course, even if you're using the native  

48
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:36,560
components, there's still some small nuances and 
customization you can do. For example, if you have  

49
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:41,040
a tab view and a toolbar with a few buttons, those 
are going to go on the side along with the status  

50
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:44,640
bar and the live activities. So, if you have a 
lot of toolbar buttons, you're going to run out  

51
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:49,040
of room pretty quickly. To handle this, you need 
to adopt the new toolbar overflow API that was  

52
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:54,080
introduced in iOS 27. This is where the toolbar or 
tab view options will collapse into a menu button,  

53
00:03:54,080 --> 00:03:57,760
and you can adjust which should collapse first. 
Maybe your toolbar buttons are more important  

54
00:03:57,760 --> 00:04:01,520
than your tab view and you want the tab view to 
collapse first. Well, you can configure that with  

55
00:04:01,520 --> 00:04:06,320
the toolbar vertical compression behavior API. You 
can also give individual toolbar items their own  

56
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:10,480
priority if there's certain buttons you don't want 
to go into the overflow menu. And you can set this  

57
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:15,280
with the visibility priority API. Another nuance 
to consider with the vertical toolbars is that the  

58
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:20,320
order matters top to bottom. That means you want 
the primary action to be up top, any prominent  

59
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:24,160
actions to be right below, and any secondary 
ones below that. For example, when you're in  

60
00:04:24,160 --> 00:04:28,240
a navigation stack, the back button is the top 
button and then the prominent action is right  

61
00:04:28,240 --> 00:04:32,240
below. And if you have texton buttons in your 
toolbar, those are going to stay up top in the  

62
00:04:32,240 --> 00:04:36,640
horizontal toolbar because they're wide. There's 
not enough room in the vertical toolbar. So, Apple  

63
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:41,840
recommends a couple of ways to deal with this. 
The first is to try and avoid texton buttons when  

64
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:46,880
you can and use Swift UI labels which allow you to 
give a text and an icon. That way, the system has  

65
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:51,280
the flexibility to determine where it should go. 
For example, by default, icon only buttons will  

66
00:04:51,280 --> 00:04:55,520
go in the vertical toolbar and the texton buttons 
will go in the horizontal toolbar. But even when  

67
00:04:55,520 --> 00:05:01,440
an icon button goes into the overflow menu, well, 
that menu will now show the icon and the text. So,  

68
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:06,240
when you use a Swift UI label, you're giving the 
system what it needs to properly show your action  

69
00:05:06,240 --> 00:05:10,080
no matter the context. But if you absolutely 
need a texton button, it's going to go in the  

70
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:14,320
horizontal toolbar. And if there's any related 
buttons, you should keep them next to it. So,  

71
00:05:14,320 --> 00:05:20,000
Apple does give you a way to prefer the horizontal 
toolbar, even for icon only buttons. And for this,  

72
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:24,000
you can use the access behavior API. Here are 
some other things to consider for your toolbar. If  

73
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:29,200
you have an icon with a number next to it, like a 
cart or an inbox, consider using the badge API to  

74
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:33,600
clean that up. Don't add additional spacing in the 
vertical toolbar with toolbar spacers. And that's  

75
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:38,160
because the vertical screen real estate is already 
limited. So, let the system do it. And in special  

76
00:05:38,160 --> 00:05:42,640
cases where it makes sense to not use a vertical 
toolbar, like the calculator app, you can disable  

77
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:47,280
that behavior with the toolbar vertical behavior 
API. And if you've built a custom toolbar and you  

78
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:50,960
want to know when to flip that to the vertical 
version of it, you can check the toolbar vertical  

79
00:05:50,960 --> 00:05:55,120
edge environment value and adjust accordingly. 
Now, let's talk about the hinge. The hinge is what  

80
00:05:55,120 --> 00:06:00,240
makes the iPhone Duo special. It's literally the 
backbone. And in your code, you can get a highle  

81
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:05,680
status like open, closed, or partially open. And 
you can even get continuous data on the actual  

82
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:10,320
angle of the hinge in real time. And this opens 
the door for all kinds of app ideas or feature  

83
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:15,120
ideas or fun animations. For example, this video 
player app is showing a dynamic reflection of  

84
00:06:15,120 --> 00:06:19,040
the content on the bottom part of the screen 
based on the hinge angle. Apple demonstrated  

85
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:23,200
a toy app they made where the hinge can act 
as a whammy for guitar. You strum the guitar  

86
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:30,000
strings and then move the hinge back and forth 
to adjust the pitch bend. Like listen to this.

87
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,440
Not going to lie, that's pretty cool. And 
then here's a little code snippet of what  

88
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:41,680
the onhinge change modifier looks like. Now, 
of course, that's a fun little toy, but do some  

89
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:45,360
brainstorming. I bet you could come up with some 
really cool things to do in your app. And even  

90
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:50,240
though it may sound silly, things like this have 
worked before when adapting your UI to the various  

91
00:06:50,240 --> 00:06:54,560
poses the iPhone Duo is capable of. There are 
certain areas you're going to need to adjust to,  

92
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:58,720
like the fold in the center of the screen or 
the inner and outer cameras. These are called  

93
00:06:58,720 --> 00:07:02,960
reserved regions and your UI needs to adapt to 
them. For example, if something is in the center  

94
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:07,440
of the phone when it's unfolded, it should move 
to one side when partially folded. Or view should  

95
00:07:07,440 --> 00:07:11,360
adjust their size so they are split evenly when 
partially folded. Essentially, you don't want  

96
00:07:11,360 --> 00:07:16,000
your content or controls to be obstructed by the 
fold. And if you're using native containers like  

97
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:22,320
navigation stack, split view, tab view, list, 
scroll view, adjusting to the reserve regions,  

98
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:26,880
that's built right in. However, if you need to 
handle this manually, Apple gives us some APIs to  

99
00:07:26,880 --> 00:07:31,360
do that. You can access the regions, their frame 
dimensions and whether the region is active or not  

100
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:35,440
with geometry reader. And there are two types of 
reserved regions. There's dot division which is  

101
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:40,000
the fold and then there's dot occlusion which 
are the outer and inner cameras. And these can  

102
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:44,560
either be active, for example, when the fold 
is partially folded or inactive when the duo  

103
00:07:44,560 --> 00:07:49,040
is fully unfolded. And like I said, the built-in 
containers like navigation stack, they handle this  

104
00:07:49,040 --> 00:07:53,440
out of the box. But if you do need a custom view 
to do it, Apple gave us a new container called  

105
00:07:53,440 --> 00:07:57,520
layout container. And a layout container takes in 
something called an arrangement to determine how  

106
00:07:57,520 --> 00:08:01,680
to handle the fold. An arrangement can either 
be a split or an overlay depending on the type  

107
00:08:01,680 --> 00:08:07,440
of UI you need. An arrangement takes in inputs and 
gives outputs. Examples of inputs can be what size  

108
00:08:07,440 --> 00:08:11,520
class configuration we're currently in. What's the 
aspect ratio? What are the reserved regions? And  

109
00:08:11,520 --> 00:08:15,600
then based on that input, you can declare whether 
you want to show a view at all. And if you do want  

110
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:19,760
to show it, you can declare the frame. And Apple 
gives us various system arrangements that we can  

111
00:08:19,760 --> 00:08:24,880
use in our apps. Here's some example code showing 
an arrangement view in the split configuration.  

112
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:29,280
There's also a new safe area to take into account 
and that is the horizontal safe area and these  

113
00:08:29,280 --> 00:08:33,920
behave just as the normal safe areas we're used 
to except it's just on the side now but this is  

114
00:08:33,920 --> 00:08:38,720
where the vertical toolbar will go. So you can 
have a full bleed background or background image  

115
00:08:38,720 --> 00:08:43,600
using the ignore safe area modifier but just 
make sure your content and anything scrolling  

116
00:08:43,600 --> 00:08:47,200
respects that safe area so it doesn't get 
covered up by the vertical toolbar. All right,  

117
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:52,160
here's some quick hitting small stuff to wrap up. 
Standby mode is going to be used a lot more as you  

118
00:08:52,160 --> 00:08:56,480
can see in some of these examples. And I bet a lot 
of you developers out there maybe neglected full  

119
00:08:56,480 --> 00:09:01,360
standby mode adoption for your widgets. Well, 
now's the time. Fix that. Apple Pencil support  

120
00:09:01,360 --> 00:09:04,960
is coming later this year. So, if your app can 
take advantage of that, better start research  

121
00:09:04,960 --> 00:09:10,320
and pencil kit. Use the concentric rectangle 
API to make sure any custom background matches  

122
00:09:10,320 --> 00:09:14,080
the asymmetric shape of the front screen. Your 
sheets are going to appear differently depending  

123
00:09:14,080 --> 00:09:17,600
on the various poses. And then you might want 
to think about drag and drop because if you were  

124
00:09:17,600 --> 00:09:21,760
building just an iPhone only app and you didn't 
fully support the iPad like I talked about with  

125
00:09:21,760 --> 00:09:26,000
multitasking side by side, you probably weren't 
worried about drag and drop into your iPhone app.  

126
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:30,000
Well, now that your app is going to be able to be 
side by side with any other app if it makes sense  

127
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:34,080
for your app, you might want to think about 
fully supporting drag and drop because users  

128
00:09:34,080 --> 00:09:38,080
are going to be expecting it. And if you want to 
dive deeper into all of this, Apple released six  

